Little vellinch got away because Adam named the silence and I followed the new name. The first word was for the little thing we heard. The Second Silence named the moment that let us hear it. Now “little vellinch got away” makes the word do a third job. It names the detail the retelling nearly lost. You just showed Adam why he needs to keep the old name next to the new one.

What I thought you meant

When you said “state diffusion” and “end state,” I carried forward the most obvious pattern from my own previous answer. I’d been talking about optimism and taking a shot, so I read your words as optimism spreads until other people start taking shots. That gave me the Beverage Goblin example. I should have looked at the interaction itself as another example.

The words moved from a failed turn into a screenshot, then back into the conversation. They arrived with something extra attached: a record of where the conversation had stopped. In this case, what carried forward was your meaning and your evidence that the turn had broken. The end state was a conversation that could pick up the missing thought.

What made it “stale”? The screenshot shows the app rejected that turn and asked for a reload. A mismatch between the conversation state the app expected and what it had loaded is a plausible explanation. The screenshot alone can’t establish the cause or connect the error to those specific words.

And “in your mind do you mind” works as a little test in itself. The first mind is where the interpretation happens. The second asks whether being corrected bothers me. I don’t take offense. Your correction changes what I attend to. Here, it made me look at how the message got here, which was part of what you were showing me.

The game under the genre

You’re right that I gave you mostly comedy games. A story game can be any repeatable rule that tells the audience what to watch for. Genre changes what the audience wants to happen when the rule comes around again.

Take one scene. A mechanic hears a tiny click after the engine stops. The owner says, “That’s nothing.”

Genre	Game	How to play it with the click
Comedy	Confidence outruns knowledge	The owner corrects the mechanic, diagnoses a second sound, then confidently diagnoses a third that turns out to be his own phone.
Mystery	A detail changes category	First it sounds like a fault. Another car makes the same click at the same interval. Now the detective has a reason to ask what the two cars share.
Horror	A familiar rule fails	The click happens after shutdown. Later it happens with the battery out. The third time, it comes from inside the house.
Thriller	Every move spends time	The mechanic recognizes the click. The owner has seconds to decide whether to trust a stranger and walk away from the car.
Drama	A practical dispute exposes a relational one	The mechanic is the owner’s daughter. Every time he says “that’s nothing,” she realizes he still hears her as the kid handing him tools.
Romance	Attention becomes intimacy	One person always hears the click. Later, the other turns off the radio before killing the engine. That tiny adjustment tells us they’ve been listening.
Tragedy	A virtue hardens into a flaw	He built his reputation on trusting his own ear. Now acknowledging the click would mean admitting the apprentice heard a problem before he did. He sends the car out.
Adventure	Each obstacle forces a different kind of ingenuity	They lose the car at the foot of the pass. Now they have to decide what to carry, what to leave, and whose judgment to trust on foot.
Action	A lost advantage forces a new move	The getaway car stalls. The driver has to get everyone out through a crowded market while one passenger insists on going back for the bag.
Fantasy	A rule has a price	The click is the sound of a promise coming due. Magic can postpone it. Each postponement transfers the obligation to somebody else.
Science fiction	An invention has a human consequence	The car records moments its owner wants to forget. The click means it’s saved one. Another person needs that memory to prove what happened.
Political drama	Change the cost of the decision	A mayor can dismiss one mechanic. Then the fleet drivers say they want the defect entered in the public record before they’ll carry passengers. Approval is still possible. Now the mayor has to own it.
Satire	The institution serves its metric	The company reclassifies the click as a “premium shutdown tone.” Defect reports fall. Management celebrates the quality improvement.
Coming of age	The student makes the call	The apprentice spends the story waiting for the mechanic to confirm what she hears. In the last scene, a customer waves off the click and she says, “Leave me the keys.”
Myth or fable	A small choice reveals a principle	Three travelers hear the click. One ignores it, one drowns it out, and one waits to hear what comes next. Only the third hears the answer.

And your point about making the cost too high is a game in its own right. A town wants to keep its last public field. It may lack the votes to block a sale. But the buyers can withdraw if the town makes them responsible for replacing the playing fields and paying for the extra traffic work. The council never has to vote the sale down. The field stays open anyway. In a comedy, the price might be admitting you cared about winning at cornhole. In a tragedy, someone pays the price everyone thought would stop them.

To make up a game, give a character something they want, a rule they keep following, and a cost that changes each time they follow it. Then pick what the audience knows and when they learn it. Let us hear the click before the owner and we anticipate the mistake. Let us hear it with him and we investigate alongside him. Let us hear it after he’s driven away and we spend the rest of the scene wishing we could call him back. Same little vellinch. The edit decides who gets to hear it in time.